4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4],[4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,1,1,1,4,4,1,1,1,4,4,4,4],[4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,1,4,1,1,1,1,4,1,4,4,4,4],[4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,1,1,4,4,4,4,4,4,1,1,4,4,4],[4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,1,4,4,4,4,4,4,1,4,4,4,4],[4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,1,1,1,1,1,1,1,1,4,4,4,4],[4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,1,1,4,4,4,4,4,4,4],[4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4],[4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4],[4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4],[4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"## What went wrong\n\nThe first program allowed both vertical and horizontal mirror axes, and train pair 2 shows that was wrong.\n\n- **Colour 8 (pair 2).** The shape is perfectly symmetric top-to-bottom: rows 4↔7, 5↔6. So the horizontal axis kept all 8 cells. The expected output instead removes (5,5) and (6,5), which is exactly what the vertical axis x=7 does.\n- **Colour 4 (pair 2).** A horizontal axis matched slightly more cells. The expected output follows the vertical axis x=12, keeping (12,11) and removing (14,8) and (16,13).\n\nSo the symmetry is always **left-right** (a vertical mirror line).\n\n## Rule\n\nFor each colour, treat all its cells as one group. Try every vertical axis, both on a column and halfway between two columns. Pick the axis that keeps the most cells, where a cell is kept only if its mirror partner is also that colour. Turn every other cell into background.\n\n## Check against the demonstrations\n\nIn pair 1, colours 1 and 6 already used vertical axes. Colour 2 tied between a vertical and a horizontal axis, and I worked out the vertical axis x=13 by hand. Its unpaired cells are (16,10), (14,16) and (14,17), which are exactly the three cells the output removes.\n\nIn pair 2 the vertical axes are x=7 for colour 8, x=12 for colour 4, and x=16.5 for colour 2. These match the corrections listed above.\n\nI have not re-run the corrected program; the harness check is what will confirm it.\n\n## Test inputs\n\n**Test 1, colour 4.** The best vertical axis is x=6. Row 19 (x=1..11) is centred on it. Three cells have no partner: (18,8), (20,8) and (21,5). The result is a symmetric arrow shape. The large colour-6 shape and the colour-3 shape are trimmed the same way.\n\n**Test 2.** The two colour-2 pieces are handled as one group, just like the split colour-2 shape in train pair 1.\n\n## Program\n\nThe program emits one checkpoint per colour, after that colour's unpaired cells are erased.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    out = g.copy()\n    colours = sorted(int(c) for c in np.unique(g) if c != bg)\n    for col in colours:\n        cells = {(int(y), int(x)) for y, x in zip(*np.nonzero(g == col))}\n        xs = [x for y, x in cells]\n        best = None\n        best_s = None\n        # vertical mirror axes: x' = s - x (s odd -> axis between columns)\n        for s in range(2 * min(xs), 2 * max(xs) + 1):\n            keep = {(y, x) for (y, x) in cells if (y, s - x) in cells}\n            if best is None or len(keep) > len(best):\n                best = keep\n                best_s = s\n        removed = cells - best\n        for (y, x) in removed:\n            out[y, x] = bg\n        if removed:\n            emit(out, f\"Colour {col}: best left-right mirror axis is x={best_s/2}; \"\n                      f\"erased {len(removed)} cell(s) with no mirror partner.\")\n    emit(out, \"Every shape is now left-right symmetric.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"rigid":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The first program allowed both vertical and horizontal mirror axes, and train pair 2 shows that was wrong."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- **Colour 8 (pair 2).** The shape is perfectly symmetric top-to-bottom: rows 4↔7, 5↔6."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"So the symmetry is always **left-right** (a vertical mirror line)."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"a198860f0ac09da1d962d8ed17794106e996b461edb6b208643a12cd5895ac9c","task_sha256":"5cd46b8fb58640a2106210fea4304b53831e89ed942b4741b9f4668838662596","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
